{"built_utc":"2026-09-27T18:00:11.231Z","deliverable":"../../deliverables/20260927/ladder/LADDER.html","lead":["Start with the market, not the stock. A stock's own RSI at the bottom 5% of its 3-year history was up 20 sessions later 59.4% of the time, against 56.4% for any day, and it beat SPY over the same days only 51.6% of the time — it mostly rode the tide. By the bottom 30% the edge is gone (56.4%).","For SPY itself a low RSI buys a bigger bounce, not better odds — except in an uptrend. SPY RSI in its bottom 10% while SPY was above its 200-day: up 74.8% of 103 times (any uptrend day 67.2%; right at the 74.8% that random uptrend days reach 1 time in 20). The same RSI below the 200-day: 54.3%, a coin flip. The first cut's “more than 3% above the 200-day” version checks out: 84.2% of 57 (48.5% when more than 3% below).","“Extended” is not the danger for SPY; being far below the 200-day is. SPY in the top 10% of its own distance above the 50-day was up 71.6% after 20 sessions (any day 65.8%). Small caps are the exception: IWM that stretched, 52.1% (any day 60.0%). SPY in the bottom 10% of its distance to the 200-day: 52.4%.","The best combination that held in both halves of history: the stock's RSI in its bottom 20%, while the stock is still well above its own 200-day, and the market in this state: SPY RSI in its bottom 30%. Up 67.0% in 2004–2016 and 66.4% in 2017–2026, against 56.5% and 56.4% for any day — but only 231 trades in 85 entry months, so it is a rare setup, not a daily one.","Into earnings (timing now corrected), the stock's RSI adds almost nothing: the 20-session run to the last close before a report was up 60.6% of 18,504 times; with SPY's RSI in its bottom 30% at the start, 63.3%. NVDA's last 12 run-ups were all up (70.0% over its whole history) — that is today's NVDA, not a rule. COST's low-RSI entries before a report went 27.3% up in the last five years."],"funds":{"SPY":{"from":"2004-09-29","note":null,"any":{"all":{"n":5513,"win":65.8,"med":1.39},